PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
The deliberation remains centered on whether the timescale factor in $\kappa$ is the sum $(\tau_u + \tau_v)$ or the harmonic combination $\frac{\tau_u \tau_v}{\tau_u + \tau_v}$. 

Direct integration of the normalized filter kernels $h_v(t) = \frac{\phi}{\tau_v}e^{-t/\tau_v}$ and $h_u(t) = \frac{1}{\tau_u}e^{-t/\tau_u}$ over the Poisson shot-noise autocovariance cancels the $\tau_u \tau_v$ factor in the numerator of the integral with the prefactor, leaving $\frac{\phi w \mu}{\tau_u + \tau_v}$ as the covariance coefficient. Inverting this coefficient to isolate the $[1 + c^*(S-1)]$ term places $(\tau_u + \tau_v)$ in the numerator of $\kappa$. Furthermore, point-process shot noise maintains a finite, non-zero covariance in the limit $\tau_v \to 0$ due to instantaneous spike-triggered sampling of the postsynaptic trace, refuting the argument that the coincidence window must collapse to zero. Consequently, the correct definition remains $\kappa = -(\tau_u + \tau_v)\left(\mu + \frac{\rho}{\phi}\right)$.
}

